## Construction

At the selected good fiber, write the static lower graph data as

```text
X = J^{-1} L,     y = J^{-1} b_lower.
```

The H02-by-H20 block `X_H02` is an invertible `248 x 248` matrix with
determinant `10 mod 11`. Therefore there is a unique vector `v` satisfying

```text
X_H02 v = y_H02.
```

For `w=Xv-y`, the H02 component vanishes exactly. This cancellation is the
key point: differentiating `Jw=Lv-b_lower` now requires connection derivatives
only for an H20 input and an H11 input. It never invokes the still-open H02
connection derivative or a pole-seven toric return.

The affine chart identity `M(a)c(a)=r(a)` is differentiated exactly as

```text
c'(0) = M(0)^{-1}(r'(0)-M'(0)c(0)).
```

The frozen H02-to-H11 divergence map is linear, so it applies directly to the
coordinate jet. This emits exact vectors `L'v` and `J'w`.

## Necessary source jet

If both Schur-Riccati equations held, their cancelled combination would give

```text
w' = X (b_eff-Sv).
```

Differentiating the static identity gives

```text
Jw' + J'w = L'v-b_lower'.
```

Consequently there is one and only one compatible terminal source jet:

```text
b_lower' = L'v-J'w-JX(b_eff-Sv).
```

The emitted target has `1,147` nonzero entries. Hence the constant B103
coordinate continuation, whose source jet is zero, fails this necessary
condition in exactly `1,147` entries.

This theorem does not select the required nonzero source jet and does not
validate 249-dimensional transport. It turns that question into one exact,
entrywise source-section test.

